## 1. New mathematics in this round

### 1.1 The ceiling of the shell Gaussian sector (`UpperShellBound.lean`)

Row A's completion condition is two-sided,
`b₋(m−k) ≤ Σ_{j∈[k,m)} β_{Z,j} ≤ b₊(m−k)`, and only the floor existed.

* `abs_literalOneLoopIntegrand_le_radial` — the pointwise radial majorant
  `|I(q)| ≤ (c_V²/32 + 4K_W²)‖q‖⁻²`, extracted from the integrability proof and
  now a theorem in its own right.
* `integral_radialCutoff_norm` — the exact four-dimensional integral
  `∫_{ℝ⁴} ‖x‖⁻²·1_{‖x‖≤1} dx = 32`; the sup-norm unit ball of `Fin 4 → ℝ` is the
  cube of volume 16 and the polar integrand is the truncated identity.
* `abs_cellGaussianCoefficient_le` — hence `|β_Z| ≤ c_V² + 128 K_W²`.
* `shellGaussian_cumulative_two_sided`, `shellGaussianOfIntegrand_cumulative_two_sided`
  — the cumulative prefix form over `Finset.Ico k m`.

### 1.2 Both sides from the same data (`TwoSidedShellGaussian.lean`)

`cellGaussian_two_sided_from_regularity` proves

    1/8388608 ≤ β_Z ≤ (4π + 64K_D)² + 128K_W²

with **no new input**: the ceiling is a function of exactly the two constants
that already drive the floor. `cellGaussian_two_sided_from_kernels` and
`shellGaussian_cumulative_from_kernels` specialise this to kernel data.

### 1.3 Zero total mass is forced, not assumed (`AveragingKernelNormalization.lean`)

The averaging map reproduces constants, i.e. its kernel has total mass `1` for
every background. Differentiating along any normalisation-preserving deformation
gives total mass `0` for the derivative kernel:

* `zeroMass_of_normalizedFamily` — uniqueness of derivatives, nothing else;
* `A1_beta_pos_ward_from_normalizedAveraging` — the Row A1a shell bound with the
  zero-mass hypothesis deleted in favour of normalisation.

The flat block-averaging kernel is recorded explicitly with its normalisation
(`blockAveragingWeight_sum`), mass (`blockAveragingKernel_mass`) and range
(`blockAveragingDispl_range`).

### 1.4 Uniformity over the RG history (`KernelComposition.lean`)

Composition of averaging kernels is convolution of weights. Total mass and
kernel mass multiply, displacements add. Consequently

* `kernelConv_zeroMass_left` — zero mass survives composition with any number of
  later normalised averaging steps;
* `transportedDerivativeKernel_mass` — the mass, hence the whole small-field
  smallness budget, is **independent of the number of composed steps**;
* `abs_cosSymbol_le_linear_of_composedKernel` — the transported averaging
  derivative still vanishes linearly, with constant `16π²(R₀+nR)²M + 2M`; only
  the range degrades.

This is the structural reason the Row A1a estimate can be uniform in the
admissible history.

### 1.5 The remainder sector from one source norm (`AnalyticChannelMajorants.lean`)

Five independent majorant families are more data than small-field analyticity
supplies. With one Cauchy pair `|a_n| ≤ A Kⁿ`:

* `geometricMajorant_sum_le` — each channel coefficient is at most `A/(1−Kθ)`,
  uniformly in the truncation order;
* `analyticCbetaBound` — an `N`-independent closed form for `C_β`;
* `criticalCoupling_antitone` — the admissible coupling is antitone in `C_β`, so
  the cruder bound gives a safe threshold;
* `A1_beta_pos_ward_from_analyticChannels` — the shell bound with the whole
  remainder sector driven by that single datum.

